/-!
# Subspace certificates for the exceptional degree-eight group

The sixteen binary words multiply by bitwise xor. The 67 listed subspaces
contain zero and are closed under xor. The extension table gives the subspace
obtained by adjoining any word; the kernel verifies every entry. This supports
an exhaustive subgroup argument without enumerating the symmetric group.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, *The Classification of Quasithin Groups*,
Proposition B.3.2(2),(5),(6), printed pp. 83–84.
-/

@[expose] public section
open BinaryPermutation Representation.BinaryOffender
namespace CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterB.Offenders.Eight.ExceptionalBest

